{"files":[{"content":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\n#define N_ITER 10 // number of simulation iterations\n#define DT 0.01f // time step\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f // to avoid zero divisors\n#define PARALLEL_THRESHOLD 64\n\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\n\n/* Compute the gravitational impact on one target body. */\nstatic void body_force_one(Body *p, float dt, int n, int i) {\n  float fx = 0.0f;\n  float fy = 0.0f;\n  float fz = 0.0f;\n\n  for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n    float dx = p[j].x - p[i].x;\n    float dy = p[j].y - p[i].y;\n    float dz = p[j].z - p[i].z;\n    float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n    float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n    float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n\n    fx += dx * inv_dist3;\n    fy += dy * inv_dist3;\n    fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n  }\n\n  p[i].vx += dt*fx;\n  p[i].vy += dt*fy;\n  p[i].vz += dt*fz;\n}\n\n/* Read a binary dataset with initialized bodies. */\nBody* read_dataset(int *nbodies) {\n  int b = fread(nbodies, sizeof(*nbodies), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError reading nbodie value\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n  Body *bodies = (Body *)malloc(*nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n  b = fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError reading input values\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n\n  return bodies;\n}\n\n/* Write simulation results into a binary dataset. */\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) {\n  int b = fwrite(bodies, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError writing to output\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n}\n\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n  int nbodies;\n  Body *bodies = read_dataset(&nbodies);\n\n  /* Reuse one team across iterations; static scheduling suits uniform work. */\n  #pragma omp parallel if (nbodies >= PARALLEL_THRESHOLD) shared(bodies, nbodies)\n  {\n    for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; iter++) {\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; i++) {\n        body_force_one(bodies, DT, nbodies, i);\n      }\n\n      /* The force-loop barrier is required before positions are integrated. */\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; i++) {\n        bodies[i].x += bodies[i].vx * DT;\n        bodies[i].y += bodies[i].vy * DT;\n        bodies[i].z += bodies[i].vz * DT;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  write_dataset(nbodies, bodies);\n  free(bodies);\n  return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n}\n","path":"src/nbody.c"}],"hypothesis":"A persistent OpenMP team amortizes startup across all simulation iterations, while static worksharing distributes equal-cost target and integration loops; a generic body-count threshold keeps small inputs serial. This should improve full-program time on the four-core target without changing each target's ascending source-body accumulation order.","notes":"This experiment retains the persistent-region, static-scheduling, and small-input threshold ideas from the fastest archived candidate. Correctness and full-program timing remain for controller evaluation; no commands were run.","research":{"queries":[],"urls":[]},"sources_kept":["45fb266b52cf459e"],"transformations":["Replace per-iteration serial force and integration loops with one OpenMP parallel region spanning all N_ITER iterations.","Workshare target-body force and position-integration loops with schedule(static), retaining the implicit barriers between force calculation and integration and between iterations.","Use an OpenMP serial region when nbodies is below the generic threshold of 64.","Extract the original ascending-j force accumulation into a per-target helper so each target retains its arithmetic order." ]}